        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "MaxDiff, also called Best-Worst Scaling, is a survey method for measuring preference. Instead of asking people to rate every item on a 1 to 10 scale, MaxDiff asks people to make trade-offs. On each screen, a respondent sees a small set of items and chooses the one they like most and the one they like least.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is useful because people are usually better at choosing between options than at using a numeric rating scale consistently. One person may give many 9s and 10s, while another person may be stricter and give mostly 6s and 7s. MaxDiff reduces that problem because it forces real differentiation.\n",
        "\n",
        "In this assignment, the items are the core MSBA classes. I estimate class preferences in three ways:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. A simple counts analysis: percent picked best minus percent picked worst.\n",
        "2. A multinomial logit model estimated by maximum likelihood.\n",
        "3. The same multinomial logit model estimated with a Bayesian Metropolis-Hastings sampler.\n",
        "\n",
        "My expectation is that the three methods should mostly agree on the strongest and weakest classes. The middle of the ranking may move around more because the differences between those classes are smaller.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Data"
      ],
