        "## Discussion\n",
        "\n",
        "The substantive result is that students do not view all MSBA classes equally. Some courses are clearly more preferred, while others are more often selected as least preferred. This is useful information for understanding student interest, but it should not be interpreted as a perfect measure of course quality. Students may choose based on instructor reputation, topic familiarity, career goals, or how difficult they expect a class to be.\n",
        "\n",
        "The three methods answer the same general question in different ways. Counts analysis is the best quick summary. It is easy to explain on a dashboard because it only uses best and worst percentages. MLE MNL is stronger when we want a formal model and standard errors. Bayesian MNL is useful when we care about uncertainty in derived quantities, such as preference shares, or when we want to extend the model later to estimate individual-level preferences.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are also caveats. The survey respondents are a self-selected sample of MSBA students, so the results may not generalize to all future students. The timing of the survey matters too. Students who have already taken a class may judge it differently from students who only know the course title. Presentation order and recent conversations with classmates could also influence choices.\n",
        "\n",
        "If I had more time, I would explore individual-level heterogeneity. For example, students interested in marketing analytics may rank the classes differently from students focused on engineering, product analytics, or consulting. A hierarchical Bayesian model would be a natural next step because it could estimate both the overall ranking and each student's personal preference pattern."
      ],
